  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAnother upper level vort max continues to drop south across the \nGreat Lakes region early this morning. Moisture profiles have become \nquite shallow over the past 24 hours with successive periods of mid \nlevel dry air advection in the active short wave pattern. Mainly dry \nconditions are expected today, although cannot rule out some patchy \nlight drizzle accompanying passage of this vort max through daybreak \ngiven the shallow moisture profiles in place and weak lingering low \nlevel lift. Continued northerly low level flow today and maintenance \nof thin layer of higher RH beneath weak 5k ft based inversion will \nlikely keep good deal of low clouds around although pockets of \nstronger mid level subsidence will allow for some sun in spots.\nAn expansive 1030mb anticyclone will continue to build in from \nthe Mid MS River Valley today which will weaken the low level \nflow this afternoon resulting in poor mixing. Poor mixing \ncombined with more of a neutral thermal advection pattern will \nresult in similar high temperatures today as observed yesterday \n(perhaps just a little warmer).\n\nBacking and weakening of the low level flow tonight should \nresult in better opportunity for scattering low clouds, although\nwould not be surprised if spots, especially east of I-69 into \nNW Ohio maintain some low cloud cover as guidance continues to \nhint at another weak mid/upper level perturbation backdooring \nacross the eastern Great Lakes. Min temperatures are of lower \nconfidence tonight given this cloud cover dependency and just \nhedged slightly cooler than model blended guidance. \n\nMonday should finally feature more aggressive positive mid \nlevel height tendencies as Upper Midwest mid level ridge shifts \neastward. An associated weak low level westerly flow regime will\nset up, but thermal gradients in this pattern are quite weak \nwith only modest low level warm advection developing. However, \nMonday will start a moderating trend that will continue into \nthe weekend.\n\nGuidance has been fairly consistent in a progressive eastern Pacific \nwave pattern, with the first of these more pronounced waves in \nthe late Wednesday to Thursday timeframe. Moisture return of any\nsignificance looks hard to come by at this time due to stubborn\nSE CONUS low/mid level ridging with better moisture transport \ndownstream of this short wave likely getting pinched off across \nthe southern and central Plains. Some elevated moisture return \nis likely into the Great Lakes region late Wednesday into \nThursday but with bulk of forcing staying north of local area \nthis would probably be just a very light rain/sprinkle event if \nanything, and confined to NW IN/SW Lower MI. This short wave \nwill not have a deep latitudinal connection and mid/upper \nridging should aggressively build back in for Friday in advance \na more robust upper level short wave depicted in medium range \nguidance. This should result in Friday/Saturday being the \nwarmest days of the period as broad warm sector develops from \nOhio Valley to the southern Great Lakes. Highs these days should\nreach low-mid 60s and possibly into upper 60s Saturday \ndepending on timing of increasing cloud cover.\n\nWhile medium range guidance is in good agreement with this wave \nacross western CONUS, a good deal of uncertainty arises for next \nweekend regarding potential phasing with southern Canadian branch of \nwesterlies. This will impact how far south better dynamics can reach \nand the magnitude of northward extent of better low level moisture \ntransport. Given at least large scale signal consensus in \nmedium range guidance, low to mid range chance shower PoPs have \nbeen maintained for next weekend with potentially a cool down \nback to near seasonable levels (briefly?) for early next week.",
